| Essential Self Technologies | Her proposed emphasis on sensory and embodied experience alongside numerical self-tracking. The archive includes a December 2013 entry and later discussion. | A design category and argument; this compilation supplies no trial validating it as a package. [Archive](https://lindastone.net/blog/); [collected discussion](https://lindastone.net/writings/). |

**Audit-supported study summary.** Mark, Voida and Cardello's CHI 2012 paper, *“A Pace Not Dictated by Electrons”: An Empirical Study of Work Without Email*, studied 13 workers during five workdays without email. The audit describes attention and stress measurements. That does not establish Stone's breath-holding prevalence or the compilation's entire physiological chain. [Author's publication listing](https://stephen.voida.com/Research/Multitasking); [paper link cited in the audit](https://time.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/chi_2012.pdf). The paper link was not readable through this revision's web retrieval.

**Open health claims.** Assertions about vagal mechanisms, immunity, lymphatic effects, humming, kazoo exercises and treatment benefits require specific studies and outcome definitions. The unnamed 2024–2025 imaging/ERP studies also require bibliographic identifiers. They are not carried forward as validated mechanisms or recommendations.

### Editorial interpretation: design ethics

A useful design argument can be retained without presenting it as Stone's formal ethical code: users should be able to choose when to attend, understand what a system measures, and decline unnecessary interruptions or collection. A product's quiet appearance is insufficient to establish that it respects autonomy or privacy.

Possible evaluation questions include whether the interface supports the user's task, whether its benefits are measured, and whether the user can stop or alter its operation. These are this edition's synthesis. Claims that an entire industry had one motive, that a movement never existed, or that a design idea never shipped have been removed because the compilation did not establish those universal conclusions.

## 6. Calm technology and ambient design

### Historical framework

**Checked source.** Weiser and Brown's *Designing Calm Technology* (1995) describes movement between the center and periphery of attention. Its Dangling String example gives network activity a physical, peripheral representation. The essay also emphasizes the individual's control over shifts of attention and the sense of connection to a place. These are design arguments, not measured guarantees for every ambient product. [Essay](https://calmtech.com/papers/designing-calm-technology).

**Historical reading retained from the compilation.** Weiser's earlier ubiquitous-computing account organizes devices around small tabs, portable pads and shared boards. The point is computation distributed through everyday settings. Specific prototype counts, radio rates, display dimensions and causal claims about later commercial products are omitted pending precise source pages. [Weiser, *The Computer for the 21st Century*, 1991](https://worrydream.com/refs/Weiser_1991_-_The_Computer_for_the_21st_Century.pdf).

**Checked source, paraphrased principles.** Calmtech.com attributes its principles to Amber Case. They concern reducing attention demands, conveying useful information, using peripheral awareness, respecting human and machine strengths, using alternatives to speech, remaining usable during failure, limiting unnecessary technology, and fitting social norms. [Case's principles](https://calmtech.com/).

The Calm Tech Institute's launch announcement is dated 21 May 2024. Its current site describes an 81-point specification process. The supplied audit also identifies a November 2024 program announcement. The original's unsupported claim that this institute's certification lists began in 2016 is removed. [Launch announcement](https://www.calmtech.institute/post/announcing-the-calm-tech-institute-designing-tech-that-finally-respects-our-time-humanity); [current description](https://www.calmtech.institute/); [program announcement cited by the audit](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/calm-tech-institute-november-edition-calm-tech-institute-1yu0c).

### Proposed review method

**Editorial proposal.** The original's four-layer method and mandatory shipping gates were not authenticated as Case's prescribed method. The following retains the practical idea with its authorship corrected:

| Review step | Question | Evidence to collect |
|---|---|---|
| Human task | What is the person trying to accomplish? | A concrete scenario and observation of use. |
| Communication | What must be visible continuously, on request or urgently? | Noticeability, comprehension and interruption measurements. |
| Failure and control | What happens when data, power or connectivity fails? | Failure tests; visible stale-data state; usable controls. |
| Social setting | Who is observed, affected or excluded? | Consent, accessibility, privacy and context review. |

A team may set its own acceptance criteria. This is not a substitute for the institute's certification process, a safety standard or a clinical evaluation.

### Ambient patterns

**Editorial taxonomy.** The compilation's examples can be organized into persistent status, threshold feedback, coarse presence, location cues, compressed forecasts and shared displays. Each describes a design option; none automatically proves low cognitive load or ethical use.

For a proposed ambient display, test whether normal, stale and failed states are distinguishable; whether a user can request detail; whether signals remain understandable across sensory differences; and whether a shared display exposes information about people who did not consent. The appropriate urgency depends on the task. A persistent cue may become distracting, and a brief alert may sometimes be necessary.

The f.lux example concerns **display color temperature**. It should not be described as directly changing room lighting. No sleep or health benefit is inferred here from the existence of that feature.

