### inFORM

**Checked paper.** Follmer and colleagues' 2013 inFORM system is a height-field display with object actuation and physical interface demonstrations. Its implementation reports:

| Quantity | Reported value |
|---|---|
| Grid | 30 × 30; 900 pins |
| Square pin width | 9.525 mm |
| Inter-pin gap | 3.175 mm |
| Nominal surface | 381 × 381 mm |
| Vertical stroke | 100 mm |
| Controller boards | 150, with six actuators per board |

Motorized slide potentiometers provide position signals. Microcontrollers digitize them using 10-bit converters and perform PID control. The feedback controller is digital; an analog sensor does not make the controller analog. The paper describes push-pull linkages and overhead sensing/projection. [UIST 2013 paper, implementation section, PDF pp. 7–8](https://trackr-media.tangiblemedia.org/publishedmedia/Papers/527-inFORM%20Dynamic%20Physical%20Affordances/Published/PDF).

**Derivation:** 9.525 + 3.175 = **12.7 mm nominal pin pitch**. Width and pitch are different quantities. Position measurement also does not directly measure load. A force estimate would require its stated model or sensing method and calibration. Exact wear life, backlash and subjective hand feel are not established by these specifications.

### Kinetic Blocks

**Checked paper.** Schoessler and colleagues' 2015 project uses a shape display for stacking, scaffolding, catapulting and manipulating passive or locking blocks. Its kinematic blocks convert vertical pin movement into additional motions. [MIT project](https://tangible.media.mit.edu/project/kinetic-blocks/); [original paper, PDF pp. 5–7 and Figs. 7–10](https://trackr-media.tangiblemedia.org/publishedmedia/Papers/584-Kinetic%20Blocks%20%20Actuated%20Constructive/Published/PDF).

| Block | Corrected mechanism |
|---|---|
| Extender | Transfers pin motion to a higher surface; units can be stacked. |
| Hanger | Uses deployable flaps/hooks to create an overhang supported by pins. |
| Rotator | Two linear gears drive a spur gear and bevel gears. Each linear gear can be driven by two pins. Reported travel is 315° in each direction. |
| Slider | Two linear gears, a spur gear, a disc and a lever produce horizontal motion. Each linear gear is driven by two pins. |

Both Rotator and Slider support input and output. The rotational travel is finite, not unlimited. The evaluation treats **more than 20%** bottom-face protrusion as a misalignment failure; the original's “at least 20%” threshold was too strong. Results concern the tested assembly tasks, not general industrial reliability. Exact tactile quality and the researchers' unreported design motives are not inferred.

## 8. Backlash, preload and strain-wave gearing

### Definitions and corrected example

**Checked reference and derivation.** Backlash concerns clearance between mating tooth surfaces. It must be distinguished from elastic deflection, hysteresis, positioning accuracy and repeatability. KHK explains both the functional need for clearance and approaches to controlling it, including overlapping gear elements and attention to shaft fastening. [KHK, *Anti-backlash Gears*](https://khkgears.net/new/gear_knowledge/gear_technical_reference/anti-backlash-gears.html).

Let `j` be the **full circumferential clearance** at the chosen pitch circle and `r` its radius, in the same length unit. With the mating member held and other compliance excluded:

```text
theta [radians] = j / r
theta [degrees] = (j / r) × 180 / pi

j = 0.4 mm; r = 12 mm
theta = 0.033333... rad = 1.90986...°
```

The take-up for one flank-to-flank reversal under those definitions is about **1.91°**. It must not be doubled again. Two complete traversals total about 3.82°, but that is a different quantity. A symmetric mathematical dead zone may use a half-width; its definition must say so.

This example uses assumed dimensions from the compilation. It is not a measurement of Kinetic Blocks. In a multistage mechanism, clearances must be referred to a common shaft through the relevant ratios before combining them. Cable compliance and housing motion need separate treatment.

### Design options retained as engineering considerations

**Conceptual synthesis from the original, not a component recommendation.** Preloaded split gears or opposing pinions can reduce reversal play within a specified load range. Mounting adjustments can change clearance but also alter contact. Output feedback can compensate some positioning effects without physically removing clearance. A different transmission architecture changes the tradeoffs in stiffness, friction, size, cost and maintenance.

No universal 10–15% preload rule, printed-gear clearance range, arcminute residual or indefinite service-life claim is retained. Selection requires the actual load spectrum, speed, temperature, material, tolerance and required accuracy. The original's assertion that the researchers necessarily rejected a particular mechanism for a stated reason is removed unless an author says so.

### Strain-wave kinematics

**Checked manufacturer explanation.** The usual arrangement has a wave generator, flexible toothed member and rigid circular spline. In the standard configuration the circular spline is fixed, the wave generator is input and the flexspline is output. [Harmonic Drive operating principles](https://legacy.harmonicdrive.net/reference/operatingprinciples/).

Define `Zf` and `Zc` as the flexspline and circular-spline tooth counts and define reduction `i = input angular speed / output angular speed`:

```text
i = Zf / (Zf - Zc)
```

**Calculated example:** with `Zf = 200` and `Zc = 202`, `i = -100`. One positive input revolution gives `-0.01` output revolution, or `-3.6°`. The sign expresses opposite rotation. Changing the fixed or output member changes the relation. A high reduction or a “zero backlash” product description does not imply infinite stiffness or zero hysteresis.

### Ratcheting and life ratings

**Checked catalog, printed p. 8.** Excessive torque can cause ratcheting between the flexspline and circular spline. The catalog warns that this can produce abnormal engagement and damage. The assertion that the mechanism cannot skip or ratchet is removed.

The CSF catalog gives wave-generator bearing ratings of **L10 = 7,000 h** and **L50 = 35,000 h**. They are different statistical life ratings, not endpoints of a universal flexspline-life range. The listed flexspline rated and repeated-peak torques use an infinite-fatigue-life design basis under the specified conditions; that is not an indefinite-service guarantee for the complete assembly. [Harmonic Drive CSF catalog, “Strength and Life”](https://www.harmonicdrive.net/_hd/content/documents/CSF_mini.pdf).

### Cycle and stress accounting

**Explicit idealized derivation.** For a two-lobe deformation field, counting two deformation cycles per *relative* revolution gives:

```text
cycles/hour = 120 × abs(n_wg - n_fs)
```

Here both signed speeds are in rpm, measured in the same reference frame. Neglecting flexspline speed gives the compilation's approximation `120 × abs(n_wg)`. At 1,000 rpm this approximation gives 120,000 cycles/hour and 1.2 billion cycles in 10,000 hours. This is cycle bookkeeping, not a fatigue-life prediction. Different stress components can have different histories; a counted cycle must be tied to the chosen damage model.

For a specified scalar stress history:

```text
stress range     = sigma_max - sigma_min
stress amplitude = (sigma_max - sigma_min) / 2
mean stress      = (sigma_max + sigma_min) / 2
```

“Twice peak stress” cannot stand in for stress amplitude without a defined history. A predictive model additionally needs material data, geometry, load history, temperature, lubrication, assembly conditions and a stated failure criterion. The unsourced 500-hour case, plastic-cup life, percentage improvements and 96.7% reliability figure are held in the open-claims register rather than used as design inputs.

## 9. Tooth-profile design, rack mapping and coning

### Profile families and evidence

The compilation's discussion of double-circular-arc profiles, commercial IH/S terminology and rack-derived profiles addresses a real design problem: maintaining suitable engagement while a flexible member deforms. It does not establish that all such profiles are interchangeable or that one published geometry reproduces a commercial tooth form.

**Corrected geometry statement.** Involute gearing can accommodate center-distance variation while preserving its ideal velocity ratio, within valid operating geometry. That property should not be confused with the separate challenge of a deforming flexspline. The supplied audit cites a [university mechanics treatment](https://users.encs.concordia.ca/~nrskumar/Index_files/Mech344/Lectures/Lecture_10.pdf); that file timed out in this revision, so this correction is audit-supported here.

**Checked patent.** US4823638 describes a profile construction using a similarity mapping with scale one-half from a rack-approximated movement locus. Its chosen reference point and relevant curve segment belong to that specific construction. A generic closed oval or an arbitrary half-size curve is not a complete implementation. [Ishikawa, US4823638A, description and claims](https://patents.google.com/patent/US4823638A/en).

Keep four questions distinct: the assumed deformation, the in-plane profile, finite-tooth corrections, and variation across face width. Patent disclosures document proposed constructions. They do not independently validate all performance claims attached to them in later prose. The original's precise percentage gains and commercial genealogy beyond the checked patents remain open.

### Finite-tooth correction

**Checked patent text and scanned equation page.** US5918508 addresses tooth inclination and the difference between the actual finite-tooth path and its rack approximation. Its equations include:

```text
epsilon = theta - 0.5 × eta × ZF / ZC       [equation 3]
mu = atan(2*m*n*sin(eta) / (rn + 2*m*n*cos(eta)))  [equation 5]
xi = epsilon + mu                         [equation 6]
```

`ZF` and `ZC` are tooth counts; `m` is module; `2n` is the tooth-count difference; `rn` is the neutral-circle radius. `eta` parameterizes contact position; `theta` depends on it and the geometry through equation (4). Angles use the patent's common angular convention. The expression `xi ≈ 0.5 ZF/ZC` is removed: it omits essential angular dependence. [US5918508A, equations (3)–(6), Fig. 3](https://patents.google.com/patent/US5918508A/en); [scan, PDF p. 12, printed column 5](https://patentimages.storage.googleapis.com/75/76/d6/0706397d47a17b/US5918508.pdf).

This excerpt explains the correction; it is not the complete profile-generation procedure. Implementation requires equation (4), the subsequent correction equations, their reference frames and the applicable approximation. The original's unsourced additional parametric profile formula is not retained as executable geometry.

### Coning and three-dimensional contact

**Patent-supported concept.** In the cup configuration described by US5918508, deformation varies along the body toward the open end; the patent illustrates this in Fig. 2. A satisfactory section therefore does not by itself establish satisfactory contact across the face. [Patent, Fig. 2](https://patents.google.com/patent/US5918508A/en).

The original's further references to US5458023, US6167783, US5269202, EP1319870 and later profiles remain a patent-reading list. Exact relief geometry, blank taper, wave-generator placement and performance require checking the relevant embodiment. The original's fixed coning angle, cup-ratio thresholds and face-contact percentages are not universal acceptance criteria and are omitted from the corrected technical account.

## 10. Hypoid contact, noise, ease-off and machine-setting polynomials

### Contact and operating duty

**Engineering framework retained from the original.** A contact-pattern observation must identify the gear pair, flank, mounting conditions, compound or simulation threshold, and applied load. A light-load marking pattern and loaded contact analysis answer different questions.

Drive and coast identify different working flanks under specified torque and rotation conventions. Coast operation can be part of ordinary overrun or reversal duty. It must not be treated as emergency-only by definition. The original's universal statements about force direction, which flank is required, and how housings always move are replaced by the requirement to state the actual configuration.

No universal “two-thirds” or “80–85%” acceptable contact coverage is adopted. Use the relevant manufacturer drawing or identified standard edition and clause. Shared concern about concentrated edge loading does not make hypoid acceptance rules automatically applicable to a strain-wave gear.

### Transmission error and frequency

**Checked research abstract.** Artoni, Kolivand and Kahraman's 2010 study treats loaded transmission error as an important source of gear noise and vibration. It formulates an ease-off optimization that reduces transmission error and contact pressure while constraining the loaded contact region in example hypoid designs. [University repository record and abstract](https://arpi.unipi.it/handle/11568/140009).

**Unit derivation:** for `Z` teeth on a member turning at `n` revolutions per second, tooth-passing frequency is `f = Z*n` hertz. If speed is supplied in rpm:

```text
f_mesh [Hz] = Z × n_rpm / 60
```

This computes a frequency, not sound amplitude. Noise also depends on excitation, load and the system's response and transmission paths. The compilation's unreferenced cabin-frequency range, individual harmonic case studies and torque percentages are not treated as general results. Distinguish sustained mesh-order tones, repeated contact-loss impacts and a transient driveline clunk when defining a measurement.

### Ease-off as a design variable

**Conceptual synthesis.** An ease-off representation describes a flank mismatch relative to a defined conjugate reference, with stated registration and sign conventions. It can provide a useful design space for contact analysis and manufacturing corrections.

Equal numerical maps alone do not establish equal stress, efficiency, noise or durability. Geometry, load, stiffness, material, friction, supports and the contact model also matter. Mapping an error to one member may be a useful correction strategy under a particular model; it does not prove that every gear pair should be corrected on the pinion alone.

A proposed workflow is to define target behavior over the intended load range, optimize within contact and manufacturing constraints, identify feasible machine changes, measure the resulting surfaces, and compare predicted and measured behavior. The required evidence is specific to the design, rather than a universal percentage improvement.

### Universal Motion Concept and a defined polynomial example

**Source-supported method; editorial notation.** Gleason describes Universal Motion Concept in its gear-technology material. The supplied audit also identifies a published flank-error compensation study as support for polynomial machine-setting methods. These sources do not validate every coefficient formula or machine-axis list in the original compilation. [Gleason, section 18.2](https://www.gleason.com/gear-technology-solutions/files/basic-html/page252.html); [study identified by the audit](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0094114X16306085). The latter page was unavailable in this revision.

The following is a **generic mathematical model**, not a vendor coefficient convention:

```text
s(q) = a0 + a1*q + a2*q^2 + ... + aK*q^K
```

Define `q` as a generating angle in radians, its zero and permitted interval, and `s` as one particular machine setting. If `s` is in millimeters, `ak` has units mm/rad^k; for an angular setting, use the corresponding angular units. In this ordinary-power convention, `s^(k)(0) = k! * ak`. A Taylor-coefficient convention instead writes `bk*q^k/k!`. The conventions must not be mixed.

For piecewise intervals, specify continuity at each boundary. Smoothness in `q` is not enough by itself: with a time law `q(t)`, physical rates also depend on `dq/dt` and its derivatives. For example:

```text
ds/dt   = (ds/dq) * (dq/dt)
d2s/dt2 = (d2s/dq2) * (dq/dt)^2 + (ds/dq) * (d2q/dt2)
```

Surface generation additionally requires cutter geometry, coordinate transforms, indexing and envelope/contact conditions. No production coefficient set, complete eight-setting definition, validated quietness target or machine-ready instructions are supplied by this compilation. The incomplete modified-roll and helical-motion formulas have been replaced by the fully defined generic model above.

